        </div>

        <div id="claims-vs-reality" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Ad Claims Versus What It Can Realistically Do
          </h2>

          <p>
            The ads usually show a dramatic before and after: a gaping crack in a driveway, a pour of granules, a quick spray, and a seamless surface. The implied promise is that it fixes any crack in any surface permanently. That is the part to discount. No single crack product can do that, because cracks fail for different reasons. A hairline in a basement floor, a moving crack in a driveway, a leaking crack in a foundation wall and a sinking slab edge need different materials, and choosing by crack type is the whole basis of our <a href="/concrete-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete crack repair guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where a water-activated granular filler makes sense is a narrow, dry, stable crack on a flat surface where appearance matters and movement is small. Think of cracks in a patio, a walkway or a garage floor that formed years ago and have not changed. In that role it fills the void, keeps out some dirt and weeds, and looks reasonably neat. Buyers report it is at its best on cracks a quarter inch wide or less, which is roughly where any rigid filler performs well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where it is likely to struggle is movement. Outdoor slabs expand and contract with temperature, and active cracks open and close across the seasons. Flexible joint sealants are rated for that movement; Sika, for example, rates its self-leveling polyurethane for movement of up to 25 percent of joint width. A hardened granular fill with no published movement rating should be treated as rigid until proven otherwise, which means a moving crack is likely to reopen along its edges. For moving horizontal cracks, a <a href="/self-leveling-concrete-crack-filler/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">self-leveling concrete crack filler</a> over backer rod is the proven approach.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is also not a waterproofing product in any documented sense. Some listings describe it as waterproof, but we found no published water-resistance rating, and buyers mostly mention rain durability rather than holding back water under pressure. For a basement crack with water coming through, use hydraulic cement or an injection repair, covered in our <a href="/basement-crack-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">basement crack sealer guide</a>. And it does nothing for structural cracks, heaving slabs or settlement, which need the cause fixed first.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
